Executive Summary\par
Current production control in Large Language Models (LLMs) relies heavily on high-cost post-training (RLHF/DPO), rigid systemic prompt inflation, or asynchronous secondary guardrail classifiers.\par
We propose a low-latency (O(1)) geometric steering module operating directly on mid-layer hidden states and output vocabulary projections. By conceptualizing the latent space as inter-translatable neighborhoods of meaning, we isolate semantic tension (R) from structural/grammatical mass (M). By modulating this injection via a token-level Shannon entropy (H(P_t)) feedback loop, the system achieves real-time directional steering without modifying model weights, inflating the prompt context window, or disrupting base grammar.\par
